  "kind": "quirk",
  "title": "Radix converters read empty text as 0, count leading zeros toward the 10-character limit, and ignore places on negative two's-complement outputs while still validating it",
  "statement": "Across the A-L Engineering radix converters in Excel 16.0 build 20430 and 20527, string inputs reject any whitespace (including spaces and tabs) with #NUM!, reject logicals with #VALUE!, count leading zeros toward the 10-character maximum (e.g. HEX2DEC(\"00000000001\") is #NUM!), accept lowercase hex digits, and treat the empty string \"\" as 0 (HEX2BIN(\"\") = \"0\"). When outputting a negative number in two's complement, Excel always emits 10 digits regardless of places (DEC2BIN(-512, 1) = \"1000000000\"), yet still requires an explicit places argument to truncate into 1..=10 (#NUM! for 0 or 11).",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "Measured across Excel 16.0.20527.20088 readings in jnt_w111_rounding and build 20430 radix batches in CalcFunc-Evidence; every listed function matches 100% of its full-bank witness rows (BIN2DEC 1,347, BIN2HEX 2,748, BIN2OCT 2,757, DEC2BIN/DEC2HEX/DEC2OCT 4,807 each, HEX2BIN 1,214, HEX2DEC 1,025, HEX2OCT 1,218).",
